GREEDY {"name": "gate-best", "outputs": ["The user wants a Python implementation of an LRU cache with O(1) get and put, with docstrings and tests.\n\nLet me think about the design:\n\nAn LRU (Least Recently Used) cache needs:\n- O(1) get: retrieve a value, marking it as recently used\n- O(1) put: insert/update a value, evicting the least recently used item when full\n\nThe classic approach: use an OrderedDict from collections, which maintains insertion order and supports move_to_end and popitem. With OrderedDict:\n- get: check membership, move_to_end(key), return value \u2014 O(1)\n- put: if key exists, delete and re-insert (or move_to_end); else insert; if over capacity, popitem(last=False) \u2014 O(1)\n\nAlternatively, implement with a doubly-linked list + hash map for a more \"from scratch\" implementation. That's a nice demonstration. I could provide both, or just one. The question says \"Write a Python implementation\" \u2014 one solid implementation with docstrings and tests. I could do the OrderedDict version as\n<<>>\n", "The user asks for a step-by-step explanation of TCP congestion control, including slow start and fast recovery. This is a technical networking question. I should provide a clear, accurate, step-by-step explanation.\n\nKey concepts to cover:\n- What congestion control is (TCP's mechanism to avoid overwhelming the network)\n- Key variables: cwnd (congestion window), ssthresh (slow start threshold), rwnd (receiver window), flight size\n- The congestion window vs receiver window: effective window = min(cwnd, rwnd)\n- Slow start: exponential growth, cwnd doubles each RTT (or increases by 1 MSS per ACK), until ssthresh or loss\n- Congestion avoidance: linear growth after ssthresh\n- Loss detection: timeout (RTO) vs triple duplicate ACK\n- Fast retransmit: retransmit after 3 duplicate ACKs\n- Fast recovery: after triple duplicate ACK, set ssthresh = cwnd/2, cwnd = ssthresh (or cwnd = ssthresh + 3*MSS), then enter congestion avoidance; avoid slow start after loss\n- Timeout case: ss\n<<>>\n", "The user wants a short story about 600 words about a lighthouse keeper who finds a message in a bottle. Let me think about what makes this story compelling.\n\nKey elements:\n- Lighthouse keeper (isolated, solitary, maritime setting)\n- Message in a bottle (mystery, communication across distance/time)\n\nI should aim for around 600 words. Let me craft something with emotional resonance. Some possible angles:\n1. The message is from someone who died/disappeared \u2014 the keeper must decide what to do with it\n2. The message is from the keeper's past \u2014 someone they lost\n3. The message is a warning or a request\n4. The message is from the future or from someone unknown\n\nA poignant angle: the keeper has been alone for years, perhaps after a loss. The bottle contains a message from someone they lost \u2014 or a message that changes their isolation. Let me think about a good emotional arc.\n\nOption: The keeper, Elias or similar, has kept the light for years. He finds a bottle. Inside is a message from a woman/man he knew \u2014 perhaps his estranged sister, or a former love,\n<<>>\n", "The user wants a comparison of B-trees and LSM-trees for a write-heavy database workload, using a table and giving a recommendation. This is a technical database systems question. I should provide a clear, accurate comparison.\n\nKey points to cover:\n- B-trees: in-place updates, good read performance, poor write performance due to random I/O, page splits, etc.\n- LSM-trees: write-optimized, sequential writes, good for write-heavy workloads, but read performance can be worse due to multiple levels, compaction overhead, etc.\n- Table format comparing: write performance, read performance, space amplification, write amplification, read amplification, compaction, concurrency, etc.\n- Recommendation: LSM-trees for write-heavy workloads.\n\nLet me structure this carefully.\n\nB-trees:\n- Updates in place (or copy-on-write in some variants)\n- Random I/O for writes\n- Good point lookups and range scans\n- Write amplification: updating a page requires writing the whole page (or at least the modified page), plus WAL\n- Read amplification: low, typically\n<<>>\n", "The user wants a bash script that:\n1. Backs up a directory to a timestamped tar.gz\n2. Keeps only the last 7 backups\n\nThis is a legitimate system administration task. Let me write a clean, robust script.\n\nKey considerations:\n- Timestamp format: something sortable like YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS\n- Backup directory location\n- Source directory argument\n- Error handling (set -euo pipefail)\n- Keeping only last 7 backups: list backups sorted by name/time, delete older ones\n- Should handle the case where the source doesn't exist\n- Should be careful with the deletion logic \u2014 only delete files matching the backup pattern, not arbitrary files\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\n# Configuration\nSOURCE_DIR=\"${1:?Usage: $0 <source_directory> [backup_directory]}\"\nBACKUP_DIR=\"${2:-./backups}\"\nKEEP=7\n\n# Validate source\nif [[ ! -d \"$SOURCE_DIR\" ]]; then\n    echo \"Error\n<<>>\n", "The user asks for a summary of causes and consequences of the 1929 stock market crash in five paragraphs. Need produce final answer only. Need ensure accuracy. Let's reason content.\n\nCauses: speculative bubble in 1920s, easy credit/margin buying, overvaluation, uneven prosperity, weak banking/financial system, agricultural/industrial overproduction, weak regulation, international debt/trade imbalances, monetary policy (Federal Reserve tightening, gold standard), panic. Consequences: loss of wealth, bank failures, credit contraction, unemployment, deflation, business failures, social/political effects, global depression, reforms (Glass-Steagall, SEC, FDIC, New Deal), long-term economic/political changes.\n\nNeed five paragraphs exactly. Could structure: 1 causes: speculation/credit; 2 causes: structural imbalances/monetary/international; 3 immediate consequences; 4 broader economic/social consequences; 5 long-term consequences/reforms. Need concise but informative.\n\nNeed be careful: crash itself Oct 1929; causes include not solely crash but Great Depression. Mention Black Tuesday/Thursday. Avoid overstat\n<<>>\n"]}
